N:Original JK listening-bridge and sound-choice game; an adult sings or plays the four bars while one child chooses the sound colour for the question and another chooses the dynamic shape of the answer.
N:Listen: Beverly Glenn-Copeland — "Ever New", from Keyboard Fantasies (1986), on the artist's Bandcamp, https://beverlyglenn-copeland.bandcamp.com/track/ever-new. Hear how a patient pulse, repeated shapes, warm voice and spacious keyboard colour can make very little material feel continually alive.
N:Beverly Glenn-Copeland is a Canadian composer and singer whose electronic and acoustic work found a new international audience decades after he recorded Keyboard Fantasies.
N:This four-bar C-D humming game uses neither the reference melody, words, harmony nor keyboard pattern; it borrows only the general questions "what changes slowly?" and "how can timbre create space?".
N:Published early: an AI-assisted original written for this course and released CC0, not traditional and not by any artist named above. Not yet tried with a class or read by its reviewers (Kindergarten music teacher, early-years/accessibility reviewer, second score reader and student listening trial); its workshop record is kept for correction.
