R:Grade 6 alternative-hip-hop listening bridge
S:Grand Central Stationery, CBC Modern Canadian Bridges (original course composition, 11 November 2026), CC0 1.0
N:Original Grade 6 alternative-hip-hop cypher bridge; the class maintains a four-bar ostinato, pairs write and perform a two-bar answer under clear content rules, and a conductor chooses the final drop and return.
N:Listen: Dream Warriors — "Wash Your Face in My Sink" (1990), from And Now the Legacy Begins, official video; its album words are clean, but the video runs 30 seconds longer than the album version, so watch it once first, https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=l2JVqAk-zVU. Listen for how a rapper controls conversational timing, rhyme placement, space and changes of vocal register over a repeating beat.
N:Dream Warriors (King Lou and Capital Q) are a Toronto duo who pioneered jazz rap in Canada and the UK, and CBC Music counts them among the songs that tell the story of Canadian hip-hop. The song keeps "false friends" and copycat rappers out of your space through a bathroom-sink metaphor, in dense internal rhymes. It replaces the first choice, TOBi's "Just Us In The Room" (2026), whose words are sensual; every recent Canadian rap track checked had words unfit for Grade 6.
N:Original classroom-safe words, D-minor hook and chord loop; no flow, lyric, beat, sample or melody from the Dream Warriors' recording is used.
N:Published early: an AI-assisted original written for this course and released CC0, not traditional and not by any artist named above. Not yet tried with a class or read by its reviewers (Grade 6 teacher, hip-hop educator, second score reader, privacy/accessibility reviewer and student cypher trial); its workshop record is kept for correction.
