R:Grade 7 electronic arranging listening bridge
S:Grand Central Stationery, CBC Modern Canadian Bridges (original course composition, 11 November 2026), CC0 1.0
N:Original Grade 7 electronic arranging lab; melody, bass and chord groups perform an eight-bar loop while a student conductor decides how layers accumulate, disappear and return.
N:Listen: The Halluci Nation — Live Concert, CBC Music Live (2023), from 2:30 ("Electric Pow Wow Drum"); preview the later songs before playing past 5:00, https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=51hg7fUZLSs. Listen for production-scale contrast: layers accumulate, a drop changes density, bass and pulse organize the room, and collaborators remain audible as distinct musical agents.
N:The Halluci Nation is an Indigenous-led electronic duo rooted in Ottawa club nights and collaboration. CBC describes their work as combining electronic genres with drumming and vocal chanting; those cultural materials belong to the artists and collaborators, not to this exercise.
N:This is a generic original electronic arranging loop using keyboard triads and synthesized or acoustic classroom sounds only. It contains no powwow drum rhythm, vocable, chant, sample, melody, title phrase or imitation of the reference.
N:Published early: an AI-assisted original written for this course and released CC0, not traditional and not by any artist named above. Not yet tried with a class or read by its reviewers (Grade 7 teacher, Indigenous electronic-music educator or approved Indigenous reviewer, second score reader, copyright/sampling check and student arranging trial); its workshop record is kept for correction.
